hands  so many began to innovate  [Music]  here in the mountains of colombia  staying warm and sheltering from the  cold is critical  but these garments called ruanas are  more than just practical clothing  they're a tradition that new weavers are  keeping alive  the colombian ruana is a work of art  behind a craft is firstly the emotions  of those who do it because we capture  how we live what we're going through now  what we've seen and what first inspired  us to do this  we started with an idea and wanted to  capture it with our craft  rafael learned the trade from his  grandfather who also earned a living  selling his creations in local stores  lockdowns during the pandemic caused  sales to drop  the people who would come to our town on  weekends to events that are organized  here stayed away  tourists come to buy things to visit and  get to know us  so the closures affected the economic  part of our workshops a lot  in bogota a similar situation was also  playing out with shops closed silvia  adrade a designer based in the colombian  capital wondered how to get through the  pandemic  the best option was to close the store  unfortunately  we loved the store it had been around  for a long time and was very successful  it was a sad decision but it was also a  good one because we knew it was the  right thing to do at the time  during the pandemic sylvia began working  with artesanias colombia on the viva  fashion collection project she trains  artisans in digital marketing and social  media  we talk about trends we talk about the  world of fashion we talk about  sustainability and natural dyes  different types of information that can  help them improve their products more  and more  we create a concept and discuss topics  that are of interest to everyone  and above all speak to the community  the classes helped raphael ladino learn  product photography and how to use  social networks as a sales tool  at that time  to colombia was a great ally  they gave us support and advice  suddenly we had time to learn more  technical basics about photography  marketing and design  many of us working inside shops  previously did not see these as options  because we were only working  producing  and selling  rafael now uses social networks to sell  his products today half his income comes  from online sales but the goals of the  project don't end there rafael and  sylvia are working on a new collection  i've already had the chance and pride to  see some of my products on the catwalk  in photographs and types of spaces  where you think  wow there they are  this latest collection will be exhibited  at one of the most important craft  events in the country  rather we are guiding collaborative  communication the conversation with the  communities  we work together to come up with a  product  what we want is to show the world that  this hidden beauty exists  and that infinite possibilities exist to  work with these techniques and with  these communities  rafael's dream is to make these fabrics  known around the globe so everyone will  recognize the magic and warmth of a  colombian ruana  do you have questions about covid19  dw science editor derek williams will  answer them with the latest research and  analysis  write to us at covid producer dw.com  this week's question from our viewer  carlos alves  do vaccines stop transmission of the  virus  [Music]  no  they don't but even if that sounds like  a simple answer  it really isn't that's because the  epidemiological picture right now has  grown  seriously  complicated um billions of doses  of different vaccines have been  administered now to people  in different combinations  the alpha and the delta variants have  been  kind of outgunned in most places by the  much more highly transmissible  omicron variant  well over half a billion cases of covet  19 have been confirmed and there are a  lot more many in people who've been  vaccinated multiple times  and plenty of people have been infected  more than once in other words  there are no clear-cut groups anymore  like there were in the earlier days of  the pandemic where where researchers  looked at categories like  like post-infected and uninfected or or  vaccinated versus unvaccinated there are  now  just billions of us with our own  individual particular histories  of covet vaccinations  exposures  and infections  one thing that is clear is that current  vaccines don't stop transmission of the  virus which is a big reason why experts  no longer really talk about reaching  herd immunity  if you can get sick with the disease  despite being vaccinated or recovered  and then pass it along to others  potentially multiple times then herd  immunity becomes  an unreachable goal  there's still a lot of debate  about whether  fully vaccinated people transmit as much  of the virus as unvaccinated people do  while they're infected but for me  that's kind of splitting hairs  no matter what your vaccination status  if you contract coped 19  you could potentially  pass it on and advice on isolation from  all leading health care authorities  reflects that  if you test positive for sars kobe 2  they say  you should go into isolation for a while  to keep from infecting others there's no  extra exception for people  who are vaccinated and had breakthrough  infections  however that still doesn't mean that  vaccination has  zero impact on transmission first and  foremost as the world health  organization writes because  if you're vaccinated you're less likely  to get the disease you're less likely to  incubate it and you're less likely to  infect someone else so  by preventing infection in the first  place you're lowering exposure in  society  as a whole  some researchers also think that having  a vaccine-induced immune response will  also make you on average  less infectious and for a shorter time  but as i said  that's still up for debate  [Music]  this show we've seen what new  developments have arisen or become  prominent as a result of the pandemic  both in medical and economic terms  and how what we've learned can also be  applied to other areas  next time we'll take another look at the  consequences of covet 19 for the  environment  that's all for this week thanks for  watching please join us again next time  and until then stay safe  [Music]  you
 

